WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.485
So right now, we are going to 
the top of the lighthouse.

00:00:13.253 --> 00:00:19.612
And then south of the lighthouse we have 
the main city of Nazaré.

00:00:24.408 --> 00:00:27.800
And then the more north we have 
Praia do Norte.

00:00:29.208 --> 00:00:30.510
The biggest wave in the world.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.466
"Now, here’s something you just have to
see to believe."

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:35.809
"You got to see this."

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:42.271
"The most amazing picture that we saw in the
newsroom today has got to be this."

00:00:42.844 --> 00:00:44.870
"Take a look at that surfer right there."

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.540
"Garrett McNamara is the fearless surfer."

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.583
"The wave is 78 feet tall..."

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:53.289
"...that is taller than a 7-story building."

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:54.554
"This is a new world record."

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:56.320
"He is riding a mountain of water off—"

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.520
"The coast of Portugal."

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:58.258
"Portugal."

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.469
"That’s in Portugal right?"

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.781
That moment made Nazaré...

00:01:03.781 --> 00:01:05.660
a small town on the coast of Portugal...

00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:09.343
home to the biggest wave ever surfed.

00:01:10.768 --> 00:01:13.324
And over the past decade, many surfers 
havecome here

00:01:13.324 --> 00:01:15.780
 to make their own attempts at a world record.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.551
Even at the risk of their lives.

00:01:20.175 --> 00:01:21.581
How can I explain it?

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:25.873
Nazaré right now, it’s the Formula 1 of
big waves.

00:01:25.873 --> 00:01:30.252
But for a long time, these waters weren’t
at the heart of an extreme sport.

00:01:30.252 --> 00:01:33.236
They were driving an entirely different culture
for centuries.

00:01:33.236 --> 00:01:36.000
A culture that’s changing dramatically today.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.433
[in Portuguese] The history of Nazaré will be different

00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:39.693
from what it would have been,

00:01:39.693 --> 00:01:41.860
if there had never been the wave. 

00:01:42.797 --> 00:01:44.784
[in Portuguese] Nazaré has been going through

00:01:44.784 --> 00:01:46.379
an intense transformation.

00:01:46.904 --> 00:01:48.430
[in Portuguese] History and memory are gradually

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.619
being erased.

00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:55.160
The world had no idea that we have 
the biggest wave in the world here.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.190
So yeah, it's never been like this.

00:02:12.842 --> 00:02:15.310
This one is a 9'6".

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.275
It is not the biggest one I have.

00:02:18.275 --> 00:02:21.557
It's already double size.

00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:25.099
My name is António Laureano.

00:02:25.099 --> 00:02:27.244
And I'm a professional big wave surfer.

00:02:27.244 --> 00:02:28.529
What's up?

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.368
I’m from here, I'm Portuguese.

00:02:32.368 --> 00:02:34.398
-It's getting bigger.
-For sure.

00:02:34.398 --> 00:02:37.300
I'm the youngest kid surfing Nazaré.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:41.851
Tomorrow with the energy really strong, 
it's gonna be way more farther out.

00:02:41.851 --> 00:02:46.401
And some days ago, I just won my first 
big wave contest in Spain.

00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:49.636
A couple of years ago, Tony went viral 

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:53.785
for one particular wave, estimated 
to be 101 feet tall. 

00:02:53.785 --> 00:02:57.151
But, he’s still waiting for the Guinness World 
Book of Records to make it official.

00:02:57.151 --> 00:03:03.729
The adrenaline that we have surfing those
waves is something that is impossible to explain.

00:03:03.729 --> 00:03:05.534
Like every big wave scares me.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.281
A crash on a wave at Nazaré...

00:03:16.068 --> 00:03:20.126
You feel that your foot is out 
behind your head...

00:03:20.126 --> 00:03:24.160
your shoulder is on your knee...

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.808
and then you spend, like long time 
under the water.

00:03:26.808 --> 00:03:29.149
It doesn't seem like it's too long.

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:34.099
But when you're getting kicked by 50 guys...

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:36.000
believe me, it's too long.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.629
It’s that extreme danger, and the rush that
comes with it

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:43.555
that led to the discipline of big wave surfing
70 years ago.

00:03:46.155 --> 00:03:49.209
Big wave surfing began in Hawaii, 
where for a long time

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:52.213
the sport had been limited to waves 
less than 10 feet.

00:03:52.213 --> 00:03:54.790
But in the 1950s, a group of daring surfers

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:59.898
successfully rode the notorious 25 foot waves
on Oahu’s North Shore.

00:03:59.898 --> 00:04:04.149
That inspired many surfers to scour the globe
for bigger and bigger waves.

00:04:04.149 --> 00:04:05.237
And they found them:

00:04:06.144 --> 00:04:12.296
Jaws, Maverick’s, Teahupoo, 
Fiji, Australia, Mexico.

00:04:12.296 --> 00:04:14.468
But they missed a spot.

00:04:14.468 --> 00:04:17.265
A place where the perfect conditions that
could generate waves

00:04:17.265 --> 00:04:20.416
larger than anyone had ever imagined.

00:04:24.974 --> 00:04:28.282
Storms are critical to big waves 
around the world.

00:04:28.282 --> 00:04:31.415
When a storm moves over the ocean, 
its winds hit the surface

00:04:31.415 --> 00:04:34.915
resulting in pulses of energy called swells.

00:04:34.915 --> 00:04:37.403
Which eventually become waves.

00:04:37.403 --> 00:04:41.814
Some of the most powerful storms form over
the Pacific and Southern oceans

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:45.834
which send swells to places like 
Hawaii and Australia.

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:48.392
The North Atlantic has similar storms..

00:04:48.392 --> 00:04:53.401
that send powerful swells barrelling towards
Nazaré between October and April.

00:04:55.119 --> 00:04:56.873
And there's one on the way now.

00:04:56.873 --> 00:05:00.761
Friday, we are going to have one swell that
is supposed to be

00:05:00.761 --> 00:05:02.661
the biggest swell of the season.

00:05:02.661 --> 00:05:03.892
The swell is huge.

00:05:03.892 --> 00:05:05.291
The wind is perfect.

00:05:05.291 --> 00:05:07.546
The direction is like a good direction.

00:05:07.546 --> 00:05:10.481
The direction is really important because
if it's the right direction

00:05:10.481 --> 00:05:13.193
we can have the effect of the canyon.

00:05:13.885 --> 00:05:17.143
The canyon that sits beneath the surface,
just off the coast

00:05:17.143 --> 00:05:21.258
sets Nazaré apart from every other 
big wave spot.

00:05:21.258 --> 00:05:27.361
It begins here, then runs for 230 kilometers
right up to Nazaré’s beach.

00:05:27.361 --> 00:05:29.419
It’s half the length of the Grand Canyon

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:32.102
but nearly 3 times as deep at its deepest point.

00:05:32.697 --> 00:05:35.154
[in Portuguese] If Nazaré didn't have the canyon

00:05:35.154 --> 00:05:37.491
Nazaré would be a different land. 

00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:39.228
[in Portuguese] My name is Jorge Barroso

00:05:39.228 --> 00:05:42.731
I was the mayor of Nazaré for 20 years. 

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:46.828
So how does this canyon lead to giant waves?

00:05:47.785 --> 00:05:50.468
Can you give me... two minutes?

00:05:54.579 --> 00:05:57.091
[in Portuguese] So, here we have the canyon.

00:05:57.091 --> 00:05:58.852
[in Portuguese] The canyon's head is 300 meters

00:05:58.852 --> 00:05:59.516
off the coast. 

00:06:02.732 --> 00:06:07.090
From that point, here, the canyon plunges
in depth remarkably quickly.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.308
Over here, the water is just 20 meters deep.

00:06:09.308 --> 00:06:12.666
But right over here, the canyon is 
200 meters deep.

00:06:12.666 --> 00:06:14.570
The length of a 60-story building.

00:06:15.524 --> 00:06:18.417
This difference has a major effect on incoming
swells.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.340
When a swell approaches Nazaré

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:25.107
it hits the canyon and 
gets split into two.

00:06:25.107 --> 00:06:27.372
The part in this shallow water slows down.

00:06:27.372 --> 00:06:31.957
But the part inside the canyon continues
to move fast and turns inward

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:33.696
toward the canyon wall.

00:06:33.696 --> 00:06:36.287
When it hits the wall, it’s suddenly forced
upwards

00:06:36.287 --> 00:06:38.396
resulting in a big wave.

00:06:38.396 --> 00:06:42.669
But when that coincides with a slow swell,
the result is a humongous wave.

00:06:43.651 --> 00:06:47.043
[in Portuguese] We've here almost a perfect storm

00:06:47.043 --> 00:06:48.658
of current waves,

00:06:48.658 --> 00:06:50.563
depths, and energy. 

00:06:50.563 --> 00:06:51.871
[in Portuguese] That's why this place is the 

00:06:51.871 --> 00:06:53.573
only one in the world. 

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:58.846
So, why did it take so long for big wave surfers
to find Nazaré?

00:06:58.846 --> 00:07:00.265
It's Portugal!

00:07:01.147 --> 00:07:07.235
Portugal is a small country and we don't have
a big culture of big-wave surfing.

00:07:07.235 --> 00:07:10.376
But for centuries, Nazaré did have 
a unique culture

00:07:10.376 --> 00:07:12.760
 one that was also shaped by these waves.

00:07:14.912 --> 00:07:16.820
"Nazaré in Portugal."

00:07:17.298 --> 00:07:19.468
"Where the only industry is fishing."

00:07:19.754 --> 00:07:22.592
[in Portuguese] It was a quiet neighboring village,

00:07:22.592 --> 00:07:24.306
with simple

00:07:24.306 --> 00:07:25.352
and calm people.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.498
[in Portuguese] In my family, we're all fishermen.

00:07:30.498 --> 00:07:32.617
[in Portuguese] Men have all been sailing

00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:35.026
since my grandfather's and 
great-grandfather's time. 

00:07:35.026 --> 00:07:37.026
[in Portuguese] And the women are fish sellers. 

00:07:43.636 --> 00:07:44.567
[in Portuguese] Since there has been Nazaré, 

00:07:44.567 --> 00:07:45.660
there has been dried fish.

00:07:46.751 --> 00:07:48.487
[in Portuguese] It was for people to have

00:07:48.487 --> 00:07:51.131
something to eat during the winter. 

00:07:53.582 --> 00:07:55.699
[in Portuguese] Here in Nazaré

00:07:55.699 --> 00:07:56.912
anything about fish is what 

00:07:56.912 --> 00:07:58.483
gave Nazaré its soul. 

00:08:00.574 --> 00:08:03.166
Fishing sustained Nazareans for generations

00:08:03.166 --> 00:08:06.028
but it also required many to risk their lives. 

00:08:06.028 --> 00:08:08.467
[in Portuguese] It was known worldwide, 

00:08:08.467 --> 00:08:10.085
precisely for death. 

00:08:10.085 --> 00:08:11.560
[in Portuguese] For the harshness of the sea. 

00:08:13.032 --> 00:08:15.172
[in Portuguese] Many people died, 

00:08:15.172 --> 00:08:16.337
many people. 

00:08:16.337 --> 00:08:17.979
[in Portuguese] People still came at night 

00:08:17.979 --> 00:08:20.552
to see if they could find the bodies.

00:08:20.552 --> 00:08:22.075
[in Portuguese] Because, you know, people died at sea

00:08:22.075 --> 00:08:23.479
and could appear

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:26.136
six or seven months after. 

00:08:26.136 --> 00:08:30.025
But despite the dangers, fishing continued
to be Nazaré’s main industry

00:08:30.025 --> 00:08:34.271
until the mid-20th century, when it began
a slow decline.

00:08:34.271 --> 00:08:36.108
Largely because the fish populations 

00:08:36.108 --> 00:08:37.442
were dwindling.

00:08:37.704 --> 00:08:38.848
[in Portuguese] Our fisherman

00:08:38.848 --> 00:08:40.947
was an ecological fisherman. 

00:08:40.947 --> 00:08:44.522
[in Portuguese] They preserve by fishing with hooks. 

00:08:44.522 --> 00:08:46.665
[in Portuguese] Then, trawling began to appear

00:08:46.665 --> 00:08:47.949
from other ports. 

00:08:47.949 --> 00:08:49.543
[in Portuguese] Trawling destroys everything

00:08:49.543 --> 00:08:51.662
that it catches along the way.

00:08:52.784 --> 00:08:54.424
[in Portuguese] There are less fish, 

00:08:54.424 --> 00:08:55.899
you can tell.

00:08:55.899 --> 00:08:57.376
[in Portuguese] I don't know if it has to do

00:08:57.376 --> 00:08:59.185
with global warming or 

00:08:59.185 --> 00:09:01.352
if it's exploitation with extensive
catching

00:09:01.806 --> 00:09:03.356
[in Portuguese]  But it's noticeable. 

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.633
The drop in fish populations forced Nazaréans

00:09:06.633 --> 00:09:11.833
to turn to a different source of income
which came from here: Praia de Nazaré.

00:09:14.995 --> 00:09:17.541
In the summers, Nazaré’s waters were calm

00:09:17.541 --> 00:09:20.322
and this beach attracted a lot of tourists.

00:09:21.161 --> 00:09:23.479
[in Portuguese]  I was already very resourceful as a
child. 

00:09:23.479 --> 00:09:24.978
[in Portuguese] Always here on the beach. 

00:09:24.978 --> 00:09:26.978
[in Portuguese] I would hold out my hand and say, 

00:09:26.978 --> 00:09:30.341
[in Portuguese] "Sir, money to eat?

00:09:30.341 --> 00:09:32.460
[in Portuguese] My father died on the boat."

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.394
[in Portuguese] My father would die 30, 40 times. 

00:09:35.394 --> 00:09:37.585
[in Portuguese]  As many times as I decided. 

00:09:38.098 --> 00:09:40.150
But these tourists only came during the summer.

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:43.390
So Nazaré needed a boost in the winter.

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:47.144
And they found it on the other side of town:
Praia do Norte.

00:09:51.002 --> 00:09:53.429
[in Portuguese]  Praia do Norte was completely 
forbidden. 

00:09:53.429 --> 00:09:55.644
[in Portuguese] Absolutely forbidden for any 
Nazaréan. 

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.761
Forbidden, because of the monstrous waves
that launch out of the canyon

00:09:59.761 --> 00:10:01.350
 here in the winter.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.100
But that changed in the early 2000s, when
some locals

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.397
who had been bodysurfing in these waters

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:08.891
recognized the the thrill
in riding these waves.

00:10:08.891 --> 00:10:12.472
And it inspired them to look for someone who
was willing to try to surf them.

00:10:12.472 --> 00:10:15.143
[in Portuguese] There's a guy who surfed a wave, 

00:10:15.143 --> 00:10:18.504
who waited for a glacier to fall 

00:10:18.504 --> 00:10:19.931
to surf the wave.   

00:10:23.809 --> 00:10:25.619
That man was Garrett MacNamara.

00:10:25.619 --> 00:10:27.206
They invited him to Nazaré.

00:10:27.206 --> 00:10:31.057
And after a year of training, McNamara caught
the 78 foot wave

00:10:31.057 --> 00:10:32.934
that set the world record in 2011.

00:10:32.934 --> 00:10:35.495
I was seeing Garrett and I was like...

00:10:35.495 --> 00:10:37.118
"Wow!".

00:10:37.939 --> 00:10:39.500
[in Portuguese] I thought it was impossible 

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:40.951
for a guy to get into those big waves. 

00:10:40.951 --> 00:10:42.808
[in Portuguese] We saw that there was an opportunity,

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.474
we tried to take advantage

00:10:44.474 --> 00:10:46.304
of that opportunity. 

00:10:46.304 --> 00:10:50.116
Over the next few years, big-wave surfers
increasingly came to Nazaré.

00:10:50.116 --> 00:10:54.539
In 2017, Brazilian surfer Rodrigo Koxa beat
McNamara’s record

00:10:54.539 --> 00:10:56.514
by surfing this 80 foot wave.

00:10:57.588 --> 00:11:01.674
And in 2018, another Brazilian, Maya Gabeira 
set the women’s world-record

00:11:01.674 --> 00:11:04.967
here on this 68 foot wave.

00:11:04.967 --> 00:11:06.697
Then set it again in 2020.

00:11:07.532 --> 00:11:12.289
Today, 5 out of the 6 biggest waves ever surfed
happened at Nazaré.

00:11:12.289 --> 00:11:15.327
And it’s led to a rise in tourism, year
by year.

00:11:15.327 --> 00:11:19.719
Driven by people visiting in the winter to
watch big wave surfing.

00:11:19.719 --> 00:11:24.000
In less than a decade, Nazaré became the
new capital of big-wave surfing.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.280
But all this success has come with complications.

00:11:34.101 --> 00:11:37.629
[in Portuguese] This used to be a fiber, 

00:11:37.629 --> 00:11:39.128
ship building, 

00:11:39.128 --> 00:11:41.653
and repair shop. 

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.509
[in Portuguese] And then surfing came. 

00:11:45.509 --> 00:11:47.509
[in Portuguese] All this is gone. 

00:11:48.588 --> 00:11:50.126
This is João Delgado.

00:11:50.126 --> 00:11:53.217
A former fisherman who’s now, a politician.

00:11:53.217 --> 00:11:58.271
[in Portuguese] Nowadays, people are coming to 
Nazaré

00:11:58.271 --> 00:12:01.109
from foreign countries

00:12:01.109 --> 00:12:03.405
who can pay several hundreds of 
thousands

00:12:03.405 --> 00:12:05.265
to buy houses in these places. 

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:09.626
[in Portuguese] Something that is practically
inconceivable

00:12:09.626 --> 00:12:12.914
for the average family living in Nazaré.

00:12:12.914 --> 00:12:20.204
[in Portuguese] Many younger families are leaving our
community. 

00:12:21.384 --> 00:12:25.689
[in Portuguese] Our concern is that Nazaré doesn't
become

00:12:25.689 --> 00:12:30.624
one of those examples that most
typically defines mass tourism, 

00:12:30.624 --> 00:12:33.626
where the culture fades

00:12:33.626 --> 00:12:35.960
and disappears as a result of 

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:39.058
this invasion of visitors. 

00:12:41.009 --> 00:12:43.658
Tomorrow, here at the lighthouse, 
is going to be crazy. 

00:12:45.383 --> 00:12:48.309
When I'm in the water, if someone does 
a really good wave

00:12:48.309 --> 00:12:51.449
you can see the crowd just getting crazy 
and cheering.

00:12:52.332 --> 00:12:54.323
Honestly, I feel nervous.

00:12:54.323 --> 00:12:59.532
But I feel ready and I'm confident in the
people with I'm surfing, with my boards

00:12:59.532 --> 00:13:02.092
with my surf, and with my voice.

00:13:02.092 --> 00:13:05.479
I just want to go out there and do the best
performance possible.

00:13:22.037 --> 00:13:25.014
[in Portuguese] This is it, it's a swell day. 

00:13:25.014 --> 00:13:27.756
[in Portuguese] At the best height of the day, I'm going
to surf, make my waves. 

00:13:27.756 --> 00:13:29.446
[in Portuguese] It's the season finale. 

00:13:29.446 --> 00:13:31.327
[in Portuguese] And yeah, that's the plan. 

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.000
Well, we hope to see the biggest wave ever.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.275
-For us.
-For us!

00:13:53.618 --> 00:13:57.434
It's like a natural forest like fire that just

00:13:57.434 --> 00:14:00.674
has such a big power that it like keeps you
focused on it

00:14:00.674 --> 00:14:01.771
like it's hard to look away.

00:14:04.003 --> 00:14:05.290
You get goose bumps.

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.173
It's something really special, yeah. 

00:15:05.883 --> 00:15:08.617
[in Portuguese] I think we're done with today's action. 

00:15:08.617 --> 00:15:09.901
[in Portuguese] And, look, in one hour,

00:15:09.901 --> 00:15:11.114
I managed to catch two good waves. 

00:15:23.063 --> 00:15:25.254
Sometimes I feel a lot of pressure

00:15:25.254 --> 00:15:31.214
because I'm the young kid from Nazaré,
you know, and surfing big waves.

00:15:31.214 --> 00:15:36.000
I'm Portuguese, I want to represent my country
like the best way I can do it.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:39.769
[in Portuguese] Having a young kid that we
consider Nazaréan, 

00:15:39.769 --> 00:15:43.821
growing up, catching the waves, 

00:15:43.821 --> 00:15:45.463
is a source of great pride for us. 

00:15:47.363 --> 00:15:48.860
[in Portuguese] Am I upset by the changes in Nazaré?

00:15:48.860 --> 00:15:49.502
[in Portuguese] No.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:52.703
[in Portuguese] The most important part of our culture

00:15:52.703 --> 00:15:55.753
is the ability to change within the
culture

00:15:55.753 --> 00:15:57.872
[in Portuguese] How do surfing and fishing co-exist in 
Nazaré?

00:15:57.872 --> 00:15:59.111
[in Portuguese] Very well. 

00:15:59.111 --> 00:16:01.779
[in Portuguese] You'll see a surfer asking fishermen

00:16:01.779 --> 00:16:04.352
what they think about the sea, 

00:16:04.352 --> 00:16:07.211
if it's going to be good. 

00:16:08.637 --> 00:16:12.102
They were like the real badass, the real heroes.

00:16:12.102 --> 00:16:18.454
In some giant days, they would manage to pass
the waves to the middle of the sea to fish,

00:16:18.454 --> 00:16:21.627
to make money, and to bring food home.

00:16:21.627 --> 00:16:24.967
But right now, big wave surfing is becoming
a tradition.

00:16:24.967 --> 00:16:27.375
I'm part of that new tradition, you know?

00:16:31.017 --> 00:16:33.633
[in Portuguese] Nazaréan culture will never be lost. 

00:16:33.633 --> 00:16:37.422
[in Portuguese] No, the Nazaréan culture is rooted in 
people. 

00:16:38.513 --> 00:16:42.801
[in Portuguese] Our heritage isn't what people come
here to do.

00:16:42.801 --> 00:16:46.018
It's what we have here. 

00:16:47.139 --> 00:16:49.732
[in Portuguese] Here the sea is inside you, 

00:16:49.732 --> 00:16:50.897
it's inside people. 

